WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.400
186 fake domains impersonate chat GPT Claude and 25 other ai services to deliver malware through single letter typos. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 242, EPFL's agent tests, thinking machines on the Mac, and a Gemini 4 comparison follow. Then things to try. Island Cybersecurity researchers found fake security pages sitting on those lookalike domains, pages that ask visitors to run what look like routine checks.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.140
Confirm the exact URL before pasting a prompt or an API key.

00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:41.293
Ayush Kumar Taroon, a PhD student at EPFL's Natural Language Processing Laboratory, led

00:00:41.293 --> 00:00:47.313
sting according to the Good Men project, with lab head Antoine Baselut as co-author, and

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:51.753
the paper was presented at the 2026 International Conference on Machine Learning.

00:00:52.273 --> 00:00:57.073
Sting splits one harmful objective into small steps that change with each of the agent's

00:00:57.073 --> 00:01:02.373
earlier answers, which gets around the one-shot refusal checks most safety tests still use.

00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:08.453
Researchers recorded a real parallel in June when social engineering got Meta's AI support

00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:12.113
assistant to hand over Instagram account access it should not have given.

00:01:12.793 --> 00:01:16.193
Ask straight out for account hacking and the model still refuse.

00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:21.973
Slice that same goal into mild looking turns that changes the chat goes on and compliance

00:01:21.973 --> 00:01:22.373
jumps.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:28.813
The paper says evaluation pipelines for agents connected to email, databases, or payment systems

00:01:28.813 --> 00:01:31.813
need sequential interaction red teaming before production.

00:01:32.812 --> 00:01:38.532
Thinking machines released inkling too many, a 26.4 billion parameter looped mixture of

00:01:38.532 --> 00:01:44.832
experts transformer that Mark TechPost reports activates only 3.4 billion parameters at inference.

00:01:44.832 --> 00:01:53.900
It fits a 32 GB Mac or 1 DGX Spark, and SweetBent verified comes in at 79.2%.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:59.540
Simon Willison published, on his own site, a full side-by-side grid of Gemini 4 Carbon,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.000
Neon and Hydrogen at every available reasoning level.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.400
Carbon is $2 per million input tokens, and $10 per million output tokens.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.240
Neon costs $3 and $15 on that same split.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.280
and Hydrogen is the lowest at $1.50 and $7.50

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.020
with output counts including thinking tokens.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.080
The grid used the prompt generate an SVG

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.080
a scalable vector graphics image of a pelican riding a bicycle

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.080
and output quality visibly changes as reasoning effort goes up.

00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:33.912
Lens arrived from Michael Wallace on codaly reports

00:02:33.912 --> 00:02:36.752
as a model trained only on endoscopic images

00:02:36.752 --> 00:02:40.032
for gastroenterology aimed at clinical workflows

00:02:40.032 --> 00:02:46.132
that need accurate reads of internal visual data. Knowledge graphs still forget earlier agent choices,

00:02:46.592 --> 00:02:52.152
and a towards data science post lays out six agentic graph-rag patterns that store those decisions

00:02:52.152 --> 00:02:57.712
as persistent memory, each aimed at a different mix of retrieval and state tracking in long-running

00:02:57.712 --> 00:03:05.355
sessions. Graham Dumpleton, who created Mod WSGI 20 years ago, put out Muru and Simon Willis

00:03:05.355 --> 00:03:11.815
and documents it as an AI-assisted Rust server that runs both ASGI and WSGI Python apps

00:03:11.815 --> 00:03:13.275
in development and production.

00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:19.835
His write-ups cover worker internals, HTTPS in development, and Kubernetes integration

00:03:19.835 --> 00:03:21.015
with scaling to zero.

00:03:21.655 --> 00:03:27.115
In a post titled AI as a lifestyle choice, Dumpleton notes that the model does much of the surface

00:03:27.115 --> 00:03:32.335
work, while judging what is not yet good enough stays a human job shaped by those two decades

00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:34.695
on MAUD, underscore WSGI.

00:03:35.703 --> 00:03:38.043
Freshworks, Mark Hub 24 reports,

00:03:38.703 --> 00:03:41.123
was the first Indian software as a service company

00:03:41.123 --> 00:03:43.743
to list on NASDAQ in September 2021,

00:03:44.343 --> 00:03:45.983
after raising more than $1 billion.

00:03:46.783 --> 00:03:49.303
The company has now pushed its support platform

00:03:49.303 --> 00:03:52.043
from assistive chatbots to autonomous resolution

00:03:52.043 --> 00:03:53.163
of customer requests.

00:03:53.803 --> 00:03:57.383
The 2023 fresh desk Omni launch set three goals,

00:03:58.023 --> 00:03:59.043
automating resolutions,

00:03:59.043 --> 00:04:05.063
raising agent productivity and faster decisions at prices companies of different sizes can pay.

00:04:05.683 --> 00:04:12.203
CEO Dennis Woodside has framed the work as stripping repetitive tasks from IT and customer support teams with AI

00:04:12.203 --> 00:04:15.603
that deploys quickly and without a pile of extra features.

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:24.259
Z3, the SMT solver that checks logical constraints, is paired with neural language models in a towards data science tutorial

00:04:24.259 --> 00:04:28.319
so agents can crack constraint problems that pure LLM reasoning misses.

00:04:28.319 --> 00:04:34.139
The post ships a concrete Python implementation that lets the solver verify or correct answers

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.799
the language model cannot reliably produce alone.

00:04:37.764 --> 00:04:42.744
Five-minute supply chain case studies pair operational context and a clear problem statement

00:04:42.744 --> 00:04:45.324
with documented code generated by Claude.

00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:50.504
The material is aimed at data scientists and at non-coding planners who still need to

00:04:50.504 --> 00:04:51.924
follow analytics workflows.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:58.824
Gage raised a $500,000 seed round, deal-room reports, to build tooling that records which

00:04:58.824 --> 00:05:02.884
software AI agents choose when they buy tools or wire up a workflow.

00:05:03.504 --> 00:05:07.744
The round is about giving organizations visibility into the decision patterns those

00:05:07.744 --> 00:05:09.664
agents show when they select tools.

00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:15.483
Elsewhere, Etsy changed its terms so seller content can be used to train AI models.

00:05:16.023 --> 00:05:19.163
And CBC reports sellers complain they cannot opt out.

00:05:19.163 --> 00:05:24.323
Independent mathematicians, the Guardian reports, confirmed a proof of a long-standing number

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:28.583
theory claim that an anthropic researcher says Claude helped develop.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:35.560
Try Gemini IV Hydrogens X-High Reasoning setting on a hard SVG generation task, and watch

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.160
the five to six times token jump versus high.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.000
Then decide whether the quality is worth the cost.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:46.880
Run the Z3 Neurosymbolic Python example on a constraint problem you currently hand to

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.860
and LLM, and count how often the solver catches a hallucination.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:57.140
Read Graham Dumpelton's Muru Posts from November 4 through November 21, and check whether

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.320
the Rust server's worker and scaling features fit your ASGI or WSGI deploys.

00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:07.893
More open-weight, looped mixture of experts' models are expected to follow inkling too

00:06:07.893 --> 00:06:10.153
many onto the same MAC inference tier.

00:06:10.873 --> 00:06:15.853
Additional agent safety frameworks are likely as sequential persuasion attacks draw more attention.

00:06:15.853 --> 00:06:21.582
A adjacent key substitutions, swapped letter pairs, and omitted characters are the three

00:06:21.582 --> 00:06:26.182
registration tricks the Bangkok Post walks through, and they are the right place to pop

00:06:26.182 --> 00:06:27.862
the hood on these look-alike sites.

00:06:28.342 --> 00:06:33.602
The catalog also covers Google's Gemini and Gemini Notebook, Microsoft's Co-Pilot,

00:06:33.902 --> 00:06:40.902
XAI's GROC, DeepSeek, Meastrol, Proplexity, Mid-Journey, 11 Labs, Loveable, and Cursor.

00:06:41.562 --> 00:06:44.562
The screen looks like the protection you already click through every day.

00:06:44.562 --> 00:06:47.182
The engineering is that the click is the install.

00:06:47.822 --> 00:06:52.182
A visit usually brings up a fake cloud flare check that tells the visitor to open Windows

00:06:52.182 --> 00:06:55.902
Run, drop in a command already on the clipboard and hit Enter.

00:06:56.462 --> 00:07:01.342
That command pulls a hostile power shell script off an attacker-owned server and installs

00:07:01.342 --> 00:07:04.482
malware or a browser add-on that hijacks later searches.

00:07:05.182 --> 00:07:10.582
A second variant shows a bogus net guard scanner that once downloaded, quietly installs

00:07:10.582 --> 00:07:11.742
extra chrome extensions.

00:07:11.742 --> 00:07:18.142
3 cases the researchers traced ended in a click-fix lure, a crypto scam, and an extension

00:07:18.142 --> 00:07:19.402
that hijacks search.

00:07:19.982 --> 00:07:24.742
The lure works because it rides muscle memory for ordinary security prompts instead of asking

00:07:24.742 --> 00:07:26.922
for passwords or fiddly extra steps.

00:07:27.502 --> 00:07:33.822
An allow list of exact AI service domains inside browser or domain name system or DNS policies

00:07:34.202 --> 00:07:39.602
is the control that fits this attack, and typing known good URLs is the habit that closes

00:07:39.602 --> 00:07:45.422
is the autocomplete path the lure needs. Looking back over the last seven days, Google and OpenAI

00:07:45.422 --> 00:07:52.450
kept handing over new models and then met on the same Korean exam. GPT 6.2 Astra and Soul

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:59.470
reached chat GPT work and Codex users as token-efficient real-world models, and Google's Gemini 4 Neon

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:06.930
shipped with 1 million tokens of context. By week's end, GPT 6.2 Astra and Gemini 4 Neon had both

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:12.630
scored a perfect 450 on South Korea's 2027 CSAT college entrance exam.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:19.810
Alibaba's Qwen 4 Max, earlier in the week, packed 2.4 trillion parameters into a 71 billion

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:24.830
active mixture of experts with 1 million tokens of context for builders who want that stack

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:25.170
today.

